"<p>The word “agent” can make a software proposal sound more capable, but not every workflow benefits from an AI system that plans and chooses actions at runtime. Many business processes are already well understood and can be handled more reliably with ordinary application code, rules, or a fixed workflow.</p>\n<p>Before adding an agent, write down the steps, decisions, exceptions, and systems involved. This often reveals which parts are predictable and which genuinely require flexible reasoning.</p>\n<h2>Use deterministic automation for known paths</h2>\n<p>If a process has stable rules and a small set of expected inputs, a regular workflow is usually easier to test, explain, and maintain. For example, routing a request based on an explicit category or checking whether a required field is present does not need a language model to decide each time.</p>\n<p>Deterministic code is not less sophisticated. It makes the system’s behavior explicit and leaves fewer opportunities for an unexpected action.</p>\n<h2>Use an agent where flexibility earns its cost</h2>\n<p>An agent may be useful when the task involves interpreting varied language, gathering information from several approved sources, or deciding which bounded tool to use next. Even then, model judgment should sit inside a predictable application boundary: known inputs, narrow tools, validated outputs, authorization checks, and a clear stopping condition.</p>\n<p>A practical design often combines both approaches. Code enforces permissions and limits; a model handles the flexible interpretation; deterministic checks verify the result before an action proceeds.</p>\n<h2>Compare the full workflow</h2>\n<p>The relevant comparison is not “agent versus no AI.” Ask whether the proposed approach improves completion time, quality, or user experience enough to justify added complexity. Include review time, retries, failure handling, monitoring, provider costs, and the burden of explaining a bad outcome.</p>\n<p>Test with real examples, especially ambiguous and out-of-scope cases. If the agent cannot identify when it should stop or ask for help, the workflow needs clearer boundaries before autonomy expands.</p>\n<h2>Start with the smallest useful decision</h2>\n<p>You may discover that only one step needs language understanding, while the rest can remain ordinary software. Build that smallest useful capability first. Keep humans involved for consequential actions, and increase autonomy only when evaluation and operational experience support it.</p>\n<p>Good system design is not about using the most autonomous architecture available. It is about choosing the simplest approach that solves the problem reliably—and making the limits clear to the people who depend on it.</p>"